WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:27.050
Here in the 21st century, Capitalism is an
increasingly controversial word. There’s

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:32.140
a growing conversation about how much we should
trust obscenely wealthy people, and whether

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:36.090
or not there should be upper limits placed
on human wealth.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:41.530
Regardless of where you stand on modern-day
captains of industry, the landscape looks

00:00:41.530 --> 00:00:48.290
nothing like it did 150 years ago. Compared
to now, the days of the Robber Barons were

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:52.550
the Wild West – both figuratively and literally.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.229
Across Europe and North America, the late
19th century was a time of industrialization

00:00:57.229 --> 00:01:04.049
and ingenuity, and it led to the world’s
inaugural class of independently uber-wealthy

00:01:04.049 --> 00:01:08.579
businessmen. Up until the 1800s, it had been
exceedingly rare for any one individual to

00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:15.130
amass huge piles of riches without ever sitting
on a throne – that is, until industrialized

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:21.960
economies began to reward men not for wholesomeness
or morality, but for shrewd calculations and

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.660
doing whatever was necessary.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:29.380
Very few men distinguished themselves in these
pursuits like today’s protagonist. He took

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:35.659
a successful family business and built it
into a three-headed empire that still helps

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:42.630
underwrite the world, even 100 years after
his death. He spent decades financing the

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:48.030
major projects and players of the Industrial
Revolution, on both sides of the Atlantic

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.030
Ocean.

00:01:49.030 --> 00:01:51.820
When governments ran out of money, he didn’t.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:58.010
His name is JP Morgan, and he paid for the
world you’re living in today.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:03.649
International Boy of Mystery

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:08.880
John Pierpont Morgan was born into about as
good a situation as anyone could ever hope

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.470
for. His paternal grandfather, Joseph Morgan,
was one of the founders of Connecticut’s

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:18.830
Aetna Insurance Company – a successful venture
that grew and evolved over the decades, and

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:25.970
was most recently bought by CVS Health Corporation
in 2017 for $69 billion dollars.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:31.209
Morgan’s father Junius was a successful
businessman in his own right. Before the birth

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:36.180
of his children, he worked as a partner at
J. M. Beebe &amp; Company, a significant New England

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.180
dry goods importer that doubled as one of
the largest retailers in Boston. He would

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.579
eventually leave his job to take an even more
glamorous position as the business partner

00:02:45.579 --> 00:02:49.959
of famous financier and London philanthropist
George Peabody.

00:02:49.959 --> 00:02:56.260
Morgan’s mother was Juliet Pierpont, the
daughter of noted Connecticut poet John Pierpont.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.989
He was best known for his poem The Airs of
Palestine, but also worked as a teacher and

00:03:01.989 --> 00:03:03.629
Unitarian Minister, too.

00:03:03.629 --> 00:03:09.909
All that is quite the preamble to the birth
of the eldest Morgan child. John Pierpont

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:16.329
Morgan was born April 17, 1837, and he very
much enjoyed the fruits of a privileged New

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:21.439
England childhood. He was a sickly boy, who
suffered from coughs and seizures. However,

00:03:21.439 --> 00:03:25.859
when he felt healthy enough, he would attend
concerts and art shows in the northeast. He

00:03:25.859 --> 00:03:30.439
enjoyed outings with his parents, where he
told everyone he liked to be called by his

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:31.849
middle name, Pierpont.

00:03:31.849 --> 00:03:36.939
At age 11, he enrolled at the forerunner to
Connecticut’s prestigious Cheshire Academy,

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:41.109
where he was allowed to board with the school’s
principal. Three years later, he smashed the

00:03:41.109 --> 00:03:44.930
entrance exam for Boston’s English High
School – a school that specifically catered

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.819
toward mathematically inclined students who
were looking for a career in business and

00:03:49.819 --> 00:03:55.040
finance. He matriculated there in the fall
of 1851, at age 14. His father was grooming

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:56.939
him for the family business.

00:03:56.939 --> 00:04:00.720
Unfortunately, before he could finish his
first year at The English High School, young

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:06.879
Pierpont was struck with another medical malady.
Pierpont was incapacitated by a devastating

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:12.510
bout with Rheumatic Fever. Just as an untreated
cold or flu might evolve into a more serious

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:18.030
case of pneumonia, Rheumatic Fever is the
occasional escalation of an untreated throat

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:23.000
infection. According to the Encyclopedia of
Infectious Diseases, Rheumatic Fever only

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.400
afflicts about three percent of streptococcal
infections, but it is more common in young

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.750
children between the ages of 5 to 14. Ironically,
the disease most often afflicts impoverished

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:36.120
people, because of a lack of proper nutrition
and hygiene.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.730
Pierpont, who was already less healthy than
the average boy his age, was completely sidelined

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:46.720
by the illness. Thus, his father Junius did
what any father would do for a sick child

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.610
– he sent him to live by himself in the
Azore Islands, in the North Atlantic Ocean.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:57.370
Mr. Morgan believed the salty air of the Portuguese
isles would help Pierpont recover from his

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:01.300
condition; ultimately, he stayed on the islands
for nearly a year.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:07.040
When Pierpont was finally well enough to return
to the US and finish his secondary education,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:12.000
he felt invigorated by his relatively clean
bill of health. He graduated from The English

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:18.690
High School quickly, earning a reward of…
more school. With his Boston education completed,

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:25.390
Junius next sent Pierpont to Bellerive, a
school in the cozy Swiss town of La Tour-de-Peilz,

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:31.440
just across Lake Geneva from the French border.
Here, he picked up a valuable fluency in French,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.200
which his father believed would be hugely
beneficial to a future business magnate like

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:37.200
Pierpont.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:42.320
Once he’d mastered French conjunctive, Pierpont
moved on to Germany’s University of Gottingen,

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.380
where he mastered his third language in less
than a year. While there, he also formalized

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:51.940
his love of art. Many of us might have loathed
much of the artistic exposure that was forced

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:56.970
upon us as young children, but evidently,
Pierpont couldn’t get enough. He left Germany

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.430
with an additional degree in Art History.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:03.960
With his formal education finally rounded
out, Pierpont went to meet his father in London.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:09.360
By the mid-1850s, Junius had taken that partnership
with George Peabody; thus, when his son was

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.370
ready to enter the work force in 1857, the
London branch of Peabody, Morgan &amp; Company

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:20.900
was the most logical place for a first job.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:23.830
Love &amp; War

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.180
Morgan worked in London for about a year before
returning to New England. Junius trusted his

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:33.950
son as a reliable business auxiliary, which
is how Pierpont landed a position as a clerk

00:06:33.950 --> 00:06:38.840
at New York’s Duncan, Sherman &amp; Company
– the American representatives of Peabody,

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:39.840
Morgan &amp; Company.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.220
Even in his 20s, Pierpont showed a keen nose
for profits. One story from his early years

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:48.810
recalls how he bought coffee at a short price
off a New Orleans boat captain who couldn’t

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.370
find any buyers for his product. Morgan used
his company’s considerable funds to buy

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:57.160
the coffee, then leveraged his connections
in the area to find buyers that the ship captain

00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:01.510
never could. He sold the coffee at a significant
rate of return.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:06.531
Pierpont had only been in New York for about
a year when he fell in love. In 1859, while

00:07:06.531 --> 00:07:11.310
navigating the New York art scene, he met
Amelia, the daughter of a wealthy arts patron

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:17.360
named Jonathan Sturges. Amelia had her own
little nickname, and liked to be called Memie.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.610
Memie and her family were scheduled to do
some travelling in 1859, and a veteran European

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:27.500
traveler like Pierpont was just the expert
they needed. He drew them up an itinerary

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:32.560
that ended in London, then met them over in
The Big Smoke once they were finished. Pierpont

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.890
spent two weeks with Memie before escorting
her across the Atlantic, back to New York.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:42.700
In the modern dating scene, a young single
woman would probably feel that Pierpont was

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:47.710
trying way too hard, but Memie didn’t seem
to mind. She agreed to marry Pierpont in the

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:53.750
following year, during the Spring of 1860.
At age 23, it was the happiest he had ever

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.160
been in his young life.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:00.120
Unfortunately, there were twin disasters right
around the corner. The following winter, in

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.810
1861, Memie came down with a pesky cough that
just wouldn’t go away. At the same time,

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.940
the American Civil War was just weeks away
from exploding.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:13.410
Now, you might think a mystery ailment would
be easier to deal with than your entire country

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:18.540
breaking out into armed conflict around you,
but Pierpont evaded service in the Union Army

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:24.750
by paying $300 to a substitute. For that matter,
so did eventual peers John D. Rockefeller,

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:29.730
Andrew Carnegie, Jay Gould, and James Mellon.
For Mellon’s part, he felt some guilt after

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:34.360
the fact about avoiding the War, prompting
his father to write to him that "a man may

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:39.180
be a patriot without risking his own life
or sacrificing his health. There are plenty

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.890
of lives less valuable."

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:44.880
We don’t know where young Pierpont stood
on the value of his own life during the first

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.130
year of the Civil War; what we do know is
that he desperately wanted to find a cure

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:53.720
for his coughing bride-to-be. After the pair
was married in October of 1861, he recalled

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:58.870
his own successful recovery in the Azores,
a decade earlier, so he made plans to honeymoon

00:08:58.870 --> 00:09:03.980
with Memie in the rejuvenating climate of
the Mediterranean. They set up a quaint residence

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:09.640
in Algeria; soon after, while visiting France,
a lung specialist finally diagnosed Memie’s

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:11.730
illness. She had tuberculosis.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:16.330
Pierpont waited on her constantly, hoping
to improve her condition. Memie actually wrote

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.670
a letter to her mother back in the States,
saying “I wish you could see his loving,

00:09:20.670 --> 00:09:24.860
devoted care of me. He spares nothing for
my comfort &amp; improvement.”

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:30.920
Sadly, tuberculosis spared very few people
in the 19th century, and Memie was no exception.

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:35.610
She died in February of 1862, four months
after her marriage to Morgan. At 24 years

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:38.290
old, our protagonist was a widower.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:44.230
Show Me the Money

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:49.580
Pierpont dealt with the loss of his love by
throwing himself back into his work. He returned

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:54.960
to New York, where he had already made quite
the name for himself as a financier. In 1860,

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:00.520
Pierpont had broken away from Duncan, Sherman
&amp; Company, where he had clerked for two years,

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:05.681
and begun representing his father’s firm
in America via his own, eponymous firm: J.

00:10:05.681 --> 00:10:07.720
Pierpont Morgan &amp; Company.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:13.070
The biggest splash he was attached to was
something of a scandal. After his engagement

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:18.320
to Memie, but before their marriage and Mediterranean
retreat, Pierpont had helped finance a nasty

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.800
bit of war profiteering, which became known
as the Hall Carbine Affair.

00:10:22.800 --> 00:10:28.320
The affair sees a lot of money and a lot of
guns change hands, and can be a bit confusing,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.790
but the simplified version goes something
like this. Morgan was part of a small consortium

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:37.900
called the Union Defense League, alongside
two gentlemen named Simon Stevens and Arthur

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:42.740
Eastman. Eastman bought 5,000 surplus Hall
Carbine rifles from the US Government, which

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:47.640
were considered antiquated and too old for
use because of their age and the caliber of

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:52.510
their rifling. Eastman bought the rifles for
$3 dollars and fifty cents a pop, financed

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:58.080
by Morgan’s money, in August of 1861. The
rifles were almost immediately sold back to

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.850
Major General John Fremont and the US government
at more than 600 percent of their original

00:11:03.850 --> 00:11:10.370
cost -- $22 a rifle. The sale was made under
the assumption that the rifling had been adjusted

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.320
to government standard .58 caliber.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:18.300
The parties exchanged guns and money, but
the Morgan consortium hadn’t held up their

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:23.460
end of the bargain. The rear breeching chambers,
where infantry would load .58 caliber ammunition

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:28.380
into the back of the weapon, had been adequately
adjusted; however, the actual rifling of the

00:11:28.380 --> 00:11:33.290
weapons remained incompatibly sized. Over
the course of the civil war, many infantrymen

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.630
blamed these old, defective Hall Carbines
for terrible misfires and accidents, many

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:43.230
of which led to self-inflicted injuries and
potentially preventable amputations.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:48.070
Almost immediately, there was a US Congressional
investigation. Major General Fremont, who

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:53.670
purchased the guns, had a boss, and that guy
complained to his boss, who complained to

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:58.040
the Secretary of War, and the next thing you
know, the Hall Carbine Affair was born. The

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:03.580
criticism was blistering; newspapers ran dramatic,
muckraking headlines. At one point, the Congressional

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:07.100
Committee on Government Contracts issued a
statement, proclaiming,

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:11.529
“Worse than traitors in arms are the men
who are pretend loyalty to the flag, feast

00:12:11.529 --> 00:12:17.380
and fatten on the misfortunes of the nation,
while patriot blood is crimsoning the plains

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:21.810
of the South, and bodies of their countrymen
are moldering in the dust."

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:28.270
Much of this criticism was refuted, years
later, by E. Gordon Wasson’s 1940s investigation,

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:33.440
The Hall Carbine Affair: A Study in Contemporary
Folklore. What isn’t refutable is that Morgan

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.470
had made a small fortune off the deal. This
new injection of capital financed several

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:43.250
of the projects he busied himself with, following
the death of Memie. Pierpont went full-throttle

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:48.040
for his father’s company until 1864; it
was then that George Peabody, his father’s

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.630
old partner, decided to retire. George Peabody
&amp; Co. was rebranded as J. S. Morgan &amp; Co.

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:58.830
Perhaps to avoid confusion between two similarly
sounding firms, Pierpont took an opportunity

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:04.420
to partner with the more senior Charles H.
Dabney, whom he knew from his European jaunts,

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:08.020
to reform his own house as Dabney, Morgan
&amp; Co.

00:13:08.020 --> 00:13:13.930
Now, with all these banking firms, forming
and reforming, you might be wondering just

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:20.350
what it is that Pierpont actually did. It’s
a fair question. Much of what he did was act

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:25.390
as an American agent for his London-based
father, but he did a fair amount of investment

00:13:25.390 --> 00:13:29.770
on his own, too. Early on, he traded mostly
in government bonds and foreign exchange,

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:35.170
but as the 1860s went on, he began leveraging
his international contacts to connect old

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:38.390
European money with new American industrialists.

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:43.320
So, as the Civil War concluded, and American
Reconstruction gave way to industrialization

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:48.839
and the Gilded Age, no one on Earth became
more adept at linking railroad barons and

00:13:48.839 --> 00:13:55.100
aspiring inventors with deep reservoirs of
investment capital. Early in his career, Pierpont

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:01.560
was often a mere facilitator, connecting financiers
and visionaries for a small cut of the profits.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.070
But as the century wore on, Morgan amassed
a larger and larger fortune, and became wealthy

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:10.860
enough – and powerful enough – to finance
many of these projects on his own.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:11.860
Becoming the Bank

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:17.620
In the aftermath of the Civil War, Pierpont
had two major life developments. First, he

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:22.620
remarried. His new wife was Frances Louisa
Tracy, another woman he had met in his high-class

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:27.360
social circle in New York. She, too, had a
nickname, and preferred to be called Fanny.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:32.320
Fanny was not Pierpont’s one true love.
Like many of the American power brokers who

00:14:32.320 --> 00:14:39.360
came after him, Pierpont preferred long, intense
hours of grinding out work, brokering meetings,

00:14:39.360 --> 00:16:33.839
and travelling abroad. Fanny was completely
his opposite – she was a homebody, who preferred

00:16:33.839 --> 00:16:38.350
to spend her time with children and close
friends. Despite their incompatibility, the

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:43.690
two remained married until Morgan’s death,
nearly 50 years later. They had four children:

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:46.980
Louisa, John Pierpont Jr., Juliet, and Anne.

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:52.110
By the birth of his youngest daughter Anne
in 1873, Pierpont’s professional life was

00:16:52.110 --> 00:16:58.270
ready to evolve as well. His new partner Dabney
retired in 1871, so Morgan formed a new partnership

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:03.290
with the powerful Drexel family of Philadelphia.
You should probably have this formula down

00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:08.110
by now, so it likely won’t surprise you
to learn that their new joint banking venture

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:13.770
was called Drexel, Morgan &amp; Company. Years
later, when Anthony Drexel died, the firm

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:20.250
was rechristened as something that might sound
more familiar to modern audiences: J.P. Morgan

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:21.250
&amp; Co.

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:26.319
For the next 20 years, Morgan’s career seemed
powered by rocket fuel. No longer a mere wealthy

00:17:26.319 --> 00:17:31.999
financier living in his powerful father’s
shadow, J.P. Morgan began shaping the numerous

00:17:31.999 --> 00:17:36.889
railroad conglomerates that were exploding
across the American landscape. A few simple

00:17:36.889 --> 00:17:41.730
loans were enough to keep the coffers full,
but Morgan did much more than that – he

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:47.429
positioned himself at the center of any number
of mergers and reorganizations, growing both

00:17:47.429 --> 00:17:49.869
his power and his portfolio.

00:17:49.869 --> 00:17:53.990
One of Morgan’s earliest moves in the Railroad
sector was seizing a relatively small line

00:17:53.990 --> 00:18:00.950
called Albany &amp; Susquehanna from robber barons
Jay Gould and Jim Fisk. In 1869, Gould and

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:05.970
Fisk had hatched a plan to take a controlling
interest in the railroad by buying a majority

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:10.369
stake. Joseph Ramsey, the President of the
Railroad, blocked their attempt by issuing

00:18:10.369 --> 00:18:15.820
thousands of new shares to his supporters;
incensed by the move, Gould and Fisk turned

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:20.039
to more nefarious methods, pressuring a crooked
judge they owned to issue arrest warrants

00:18:20.039 --> 00:18:25.809
for President Ramsey. At one point, Fisk literally
got on a train and rode through several Albany

00:18:25.809 --> 00:18:30.749
&amp; Susquehanna line stations, beating up rail
workers in an attempt to intimidate the company

00:18:30.749 --> 00:18:34.220
into submission. The only thing that stopped
him was a railroad Employee, who pretended

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:38.820
to be a police officer, arrested Fisk, and
took him to jail. When he was eventually released,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:44.010
Fisk did the same thing all over again, culminating
in a massive railway brawl that had to be

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:45.940
broken up by the state militia.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:50.429
All of this madness ended with J.P. Morgan,
sensing an opportunity and swooping in with

00:18:50.429 --> 00:18:55.409
a $500,000 mortgage payment. Morgan talked
his way into stewardship of the railroad -- he

00:18:55.409 --> 00:18:59.529
was elected Vice President of the company
and controller of the road; six months later,

00:18:59.529 --> 00:19:05.799
he leased it to the Delaware &amp; Hudson Railroad
company for $490,000 per year. The lease ran

00:19:05.799 --> 00:19:07.490
for 99 years.

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:11.580
Morgan was already well off from his family
wealth and his early career moves, but his

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:16.980
involvement with the railroads made him obscenely
rich. For decades, he scoured the exploding

00:19:16.980 --> 00:19:22.100
railroad industry for opportunities to, and
it rarely disappointed him. In 1879, for example,

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:27.080
railroad magnate William Vanderbilt approached
him, asking for help in the sale of $250,000

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:32.809
in stock shares for the New York Central Railroad.
He sold all of it, without dropping the share

00:19:32.809 --> 00:19:37.299
price by one dollar, which scored him a seat
on the railroad’s board of directors.

00:19:37.299 --> 00:19:42.570
In 1880, he was the central figure in a syndicate
that made a $40 million bond sale to finance

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:47.399
the highly anticipated Northern Pacific Railway.
Up to that point, it was the largest transaction

00:19:47.399 --> 00:19:49.899
of Railroad bonds in US History.

00:19:49.899 --> 00:19:55.029
In 1885, he restructured the New York, West
Shore &amp; Buffalo Railroad Company, then leased

00:19:55.029 --> 00:19:59.190
it to Vanderbilt’s New York Central. He
made similar moves in Pennsylvania, Virginia,

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:00.350
and Ohio.

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:05.279
As Morgan’s personal fortune grew, so did
his clout as a Captain of Industry. In 1882,

00:20:05.279 --> 00:20:10.980
he moved his family to a prominent brownstone
mansion at 219 Madison Avenue, three blocks

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:15.909
from where the Empire State Building would
eventually be built. He was a recognizable

00:20:15.909 --> 00:20:20.840
face around New York City – not just because
of his metaphorical stature in the Finance

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:26.929
World, but also because of his literal countenance.
Morgan was tall and broad-shouldered, with

00:20:26.929 --> 00:20:32.360
a great, bushy mustache, and as he grew into
middle age, his face was actually becoming

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:37.690
deformed. Among the maladies that Morgan suffered
from was Rosacea – a chronic skin condition

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:43.090
that swells and reddens much of the tissue
around the nose and cheeks. Morgan’s rosacea

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:48.679
was coupled with rhinophyma – the development
of a huge, overgrown, bulbous nose, which

00:20:48.679 --> 00:20:51.980
often brings inflammation, pimples, and papules.

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:57.879
The combination of Morgan’s size, aggression,
and facial features made him a truly intimidating

00:20:57.879 --> 00:21:03.460
man to be around. Other titans of the gilded
age carried a reputation for cutthroat tendencies,

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:08.679
but Morgan actually looked the part. To make
matters worse, he was often photographed because

00:21:08.679 --> 00:21:13.159
of his fame, but he hated to be photographed
because he was self-conscious about his appearance.

00:21:13.159 --> 00:21:18.029
His anger at being photographed only served
to heighten his naturally intimidating profile.

00:21:18.029 --> 00:21:22.980
It was a vicious cycle – one that only served
to make him more intimidating and distrusted

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:26.340
by the public as his wealth kept growing.

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:31.950
If You Have to Ask…

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:36.570
By the time that Junius Morgan passed away
in 1890, his son had become one of the wealthiest

00:21:36.570 --> 00:21:40.840
and most powerful men in the world. Morgan
had amassed such a combination of money and

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:46.139
power that he was almost single-handedly responsible
for navigating the United States economy out

00:21:46.139 --> 00:21:51.999
of the Panic of 1893. Back when the US Dollar
was backed by gold, a financial downturn could

00:21:51.999 --> 00:21:56.389
wipe out the national gold reserves. That’s
precisely what happened in the final years

00:21:56.389 --> 00:21:57.899
of the 19th century.

00:21:57.899 --> 00:22:03.330
As the US recoiled under a mountain of financial
pressure, Morgan called a meeting with US

00:22:03.330 --> 00:22:09.629
President Grove Cleveland, offering to replenish
the US coffers from his banks. The US Government

00:22:09.629 --> 00:22:14.509
resisted at first, exploring the idea of selling
bonds to the public, but ultimately obtained

00:22:14.509 --> 00:22:20.639
3.5 million ounces of gold from a trust headed
by Morgan. In return, the US government issued

00:22:20.639 --> 00:22:22.220
30-year bonds.

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:27.429
Morgan saved the treasury, but in doing so,
armed the Democratic Party with one of its

00:22:27.429 --> 00:22:33.019
chief complaints in the upcoming Election
of 1896. William Jennings Bryan skewered the

00:22:33.019 --> 00:22:39.279
cozy relationship between Republicans and
wildly powerful banks, like J.P. Morgan &amp; Company.

00:22:39.279 --> 00:22:43.510
Bryan’s point was proven both right and
moot when Morgan and several of his banker

00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:48.600
friends donated heavily to the campaign of
Republican nominee William McKinley, who won

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:51.210
in 1896, and again in 1900.

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:56.200
Life was good for J.P. Morgan. He smoked Cuban
Cigars all day, every day, and purchased fine

00:22:56.200 --> 00:23:01.740
art wherever he could find it. He was grooming
his only son, John Pierpont Jr., to join the

00:23:01.740 --> 00:23:07.019
Morgan banking empire, just as his father
once did for him. His eldest daughter, Louisa,

00:23:07.019 --> 00:23:11.299
married a lawyer named Herbert Satterlee who
was on track to become Assistant Secretary

00:23:11.299 --> 00:23:16.559
of the Navy. Another daughter, Juliet, married
a man named William Pierson Hamilton, a descendent

00:23:16.559 --> 00:23:21.230
of Alexander Hamilton. As the 20th century
approached, life in the House of Morgan was

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:23.220
very, very good.

00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:27.549
Morgan became president of the Metropolitan
Museum of Art and was also a prominent leader

00:23:27.549 --> 00:23:32.500
in the Episcopal Church. He spent a great
deal of time collecting rare gemstones; part

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:37.369
of his collection was displayed at the World’s
Fair in Paris in 1889. And somehow, in between

00:23:37.369 --> 00:23:42.359
all that, and all the banking work, he was
a frequent, passionate yachter.

00:23:42.359 --> 00:23:47.859
Morgan loved to sail; after decades of grinding
out work, he likely saw a good retreat on

00:23:47.859 --> 00:23:52.419
one of his yachts as a well-earned respite.
One infamous story from the House of Morgan

00:23:52.419 --> 00:23:57.240
recounts how a socialite friend of his once
asked how much the maintenance on his yacht

00:23:57.240 --> 00:24:02.279
cost; Morgan allegedly replied that, “If
you have to ask, you can’t afford it.”

00:24:02.279 --> 00:24:06.139
Thus, a truly American syllogism was born.

00:24:06.139 --> 00:24:10.950
In his later years, Morgan certainly had interests
to enjoy, but it didn’t mean he wasn’t

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:15.360
still working. He helped fund Thomas Edison’s
initial experiments with electricity, and,

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:20.409
later, helped him form General Electric. Separately,
in 1898, J.P. Morgan and Co. financed the

00:24:20.409 --> 00:24:25.350
creation of Federal Steel. Two years later,
he began negotiating with Andrew Carnegie

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:30.409
and Charles Schwab to buy Carnegie’s steel
business, in the hopes of merging it with

00:24:30.409 --> 00:24:35.269
other, smaller companies. Together, those
acquisitions, as well as his own creation,

00:24:35.269 --> 00:24:40.799
could form one massive conglomerate that succeeded
via superior logistics and infrastructure.

00:24:40.799 --> 00:24:47.470
The result was the United States Steel Corporation,
and in 1901, it was awarded a market capitalization

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:52.200
of $1.4 billion dollars, making it the world’s
first billion-dollar company.

00:24:52.200 --> 00:24:56.269
Around the same time, Morgan teamed up with
Canadian-American railroad executive James

00:24:56.269 --> 00:25:02.929
Hill to create another new corporation, the
Northern Securities Company. This was a holding

00:25:02.929 --> 00:25:07.919
company, designed to hold the majority shares
in Northern Pacific, Great Northern, and CB&amp;Q

00:25:07.919 --> 00:25:13.270
railroads. The stroke gave Morgan control
of one-third of all US Railways.

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:18.190
Now, in the aftermath of the Sherman Anti-Trust
Law of 1890, Morgan was careful to avoid moves

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:22.820
that could be seen as subverting fair market
competition. He would even go so far as to

00:25:22.820 --> 00:25:27.840
directly check with the US President on the
legality and fairness of potential acquisitions

00:25:27.840 --> 00:25:32.789
before buying or restructuring anything. But
the Northern Securities Company is where Morgan

00:25:32.789 --> 00:25:38.110
finally pushed his luck one step too far.
His friend and ally, President William McKinley,

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:43.639
had been assassinated by an anarchist in September
of 1901. He was replaced by Vice President

00:25:43.639 --> 00:25:49.990
Teddy Roosevelt, a headstrong populist and
avid Trust-Buster who had a much less chummy

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:53.169
relationship with America’s biggest banker.

00:25:53.169 --> 00:25:58.299
Roosevelt wasted no time in using the Sherman
Anti-Trust Law to break up the Northern Securities

00:25:58.299 --> 00:25:59.529
Company in 1902. Morgan was infuriated – he
wasn’t going to lose any money, but he had

00:25:59.529 --> 00:26:00.529
carefully cultivated his reputation as an
untouchable financier, and he didn’t want

00:26:00.529 --> 00:26:01.940
the black mark of an anti-trust suit on his
resume. Thus, Morgan waged a drawn-out legal

00:26:01.940 --> 00:26:06.470
battle with Roosevelt and his government,
even though he suspected the law wasn’t

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:11.269
on his side as soon as the Justice Department
had sued him. The US Supreme Court upheld

00:26:11.269 --> 00:26:14.610
Roosevelt’s decision two years later, in
1904.

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:18.420
Morgan remained angry with Roosevelt for the
remainder of his life, but it didn’t stop

00:26:18.420 --> 00:26:22.730
him from helping the federal government when
it needed it… again. During the Panic of

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:27.509
1907, the US economy was once again in dire
straits. Several key banks were on the brink

00:26:27.509 --> 00:26:32.159
of collapse, so Morgan did what he did best
– he leveraged his considerable political

00:26:32.159 --> 00:26:36.799
power to get as many wealthy friends into
one room as he could, then commanded them

00:26:36.799 --> 00:26:41.679
when, where, and how to spend their money
to stop the Panic. He used his influence to

00:26:41.679 --> 00:26:47.269
secure otherwise unavailable lines of credit,
then bought up tons of devalued stock to inject

00:26:47.269 --> 00:26:52.200
much-needed capital back into the economy.
He even scored a revenge win over Roosevelt

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:56.559
– the story is convoluted, but Morgan was
put in a position where he could save the

00:26:56.559 --> 00:27:02.340
US economy by spending $30 million to purchase
stock in a company called Tennessee Coal &amp; Iron,

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:07.489
which was a chief competitor of his company,
US Steel. By the letter of the law, the move

00:27:07.489 --> 00:27:12.999
violated the Sherman Anti-Trust Act, but Roosevelt
had no recourse but to allow the purchase.

00:27:12.999 --> 00:27:19.640
The Panic ended days later.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:21.419
The Three Bank Legacy

00:27:21.419 --> 00:27:26.659
Despite his stabilizing efforts during the
Panic of 1907, JP Morgan was all but retired.

00:27:26.659 --> 00:27:31.330
Fifty years of stocks and steel in imperfect
health had left him tired and out of energy.

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:35.940
Understandably, he wanted to enjoy his yachts
and his art, so he spent the final years of

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:40.830
his life at sea and in leisure, supporting
many of the New York artists that he enjoyed,

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:42.450
and sailing to new parts of the world.

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:47.659
In 1911, the avid ship lover was excited to
take his place on the maiden voyage of the

00:27:47.659 --> 00:27:52.860
Titanic. The Titanic was operated by the White
Star Line, which was owned by a Morgan corporation,

00:27:52.860 --> 00:27:56.739
the International Mercantile Marine Company.
The chain of ownership meant Morgan had his

00:27:56.739 --> 00:28:00.499
own private suite and promenade deck on the
ship. At the very last minute, though, he

00:28:00.499 --> 00:28:05.379
decided to cancel his attendance on the maiden
voyage due to health reasons, choosing to

00:28:05.379 --> 00:28:07.919
remain at a resort in the French Alps.

00:28:07.919 --> 00:28:12.809
After the ship sank, he remarked, “Monetary
losses amount to nothing in life. It is the

00:28:12.809 --> 00:28:18.849
loss of life that counts.” It’s certainly
a credible opinion, from a man who appeared

00:28:18.849 --> 00:28:21.739
to have all the money in the world.

00:28:21.739 --> 00:28:27.179
Morgan outlived the Titanic, but not by much.
His health began deteriorating in 1913; he

00:28:27.179 --> 00:28:34.029
died while visiting Rome, on March 31, 1913.
He was two weeks shy of his 76th birthday.

00:28:34.029 --> 00:28:39.169
Morgan’s legacy as both a banker and a philanthropist
is a powerful one. His personal estate was

00:28:39.169 --> 00:28:43.970
worth $68.3 million dollars at the time of
his death; in today’s money, that would

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:48.389
make him a billionaire. Much of his art was
either donated to the Met or preserved in

00:28:48.389 --> 00:28:53.600
what is now New York’s Morgan Library &amp; Museum.
We could probably do an entire video just

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:58.890
on Morgan’s Art Collection, which was worth
tens of millions of dollars, even back then.

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:03.809
As for JP Morgan and Co, his son took over
the family business after his death. The company

00:29:03.809 --> 00:29:08.509
included Morgan’s original business enterprise,
as well as his father’s London enterprise

00:29:08.509 --> 00:29:12.779
that Morgan eventually took over after the
old man’s death. The London company was

00:29:12.779 --> 00:29:18.119
restructured shortly before Morgan’s death
as Morgan, Grenfell, &amp; Co. It remained tied

00:29:18.119 --> 00:29:24.119
to its sister companies in the US until the
1980s; it became Deutsche Bank in 1990.

00:29:24.119 --> 00:29:28.919
As for the US side of things, JP Morgan &amp; Co
was forced to split itself by the Glass-Steagall

00:29:28.919 --> 00:29:34.630
Act of 1933, which forced banks to separate
their commercial and investment elements.

00:29:34.630 --> 00:29:39.960
J.P. Morgan &amp; Company stayed on the commercial
side, eventually becoming J.P. Morgan Chase

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:45.539
at the turn of the 21st century. The speculative
side of J.P. Morgan morphed into Morgan Stanley

00:29:45.539 --> 00:29:47.029
in 1935.

00:29:47.029 --> 00:29:52.929
That means that J.P. Morgan, over the course
of 75 years, engineered the creation of three

00:29:52.929 --> 00:29:59.230
of the 21st century’s most prominent banks.
And even that, arguably, wasn’t his greatest

00:29:59.230 --> 00:30:03.979
accomplishment. His repeated support of troubled
banks, failing businesses, and a panicked

00:30:03.979 --> 00:30:08.479
US government prompted the creation of the
US Federal Reserve. In the aftermath of the

00:30:08.479 --> 00:30:13.490
Panic of 1907, it was clear that the US government
could no longer operate without a federal

00:30:13.490 --> 00:30:18.390
banking system that could better control the
flow of money throughout the economy. It was

00:30:18.390 --> 00:30:23.169
too risky to rely on the timely support of
a few ultra-wealthy power brokers to resolve

00:30:23.169 --> 00:30:27.899
recessions; six years later, during the same
year that Morgan died, the US created the

00:30:27.899 --> 00:30:29.279
Federal Reserve.

00:30:29.279 --> 00:30:33.850
Despite momentous public backlash, The Federal
Reserve was a necessary action. The United

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:38.980
States could never again allow itself to rely
on an ultra-wealthy power broker that could

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:44.210
single handedly put the economy back together
again; after all, it was unlikely that there

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:57.099
would ever be another man like J.P. Morgan.

